Subject: [PATCH] radeonsi: keep buffers in the reuse cache longer under virtio-gpu

The amdgpu winsys releases an unused buffer from pb_cache half a second
after the last reference to it goes away.  Dropping it costs whatever the
CPU mapping of its replacement costs, and on bare metal that is cheap:
first touching every page of a fresh 16 MiB mapping takes 0.8 ms on an
RX 9070 XT, roughly 0.2 us per 4 KiB page.

Behind a virtio-gpu native context it is not cheap.  Every page of a new
mapping faults into a range the host GPU driver exposes to the VMM, which
the guest resolves one 4 KiB page at a time: the same 16 MiB mapping takes
18 ms in the guest, about 4.3 us per page.  The cost is linear in the
number of pages and measures the same on an Intel Xe host, so it comes
from the virtualisation path rather than from the amdgpu driver.

A cache hit avoids all of it, because a cached buffer keeps the CPU
mapping it already faulted in.  But half a second is short enough that
ordinary desktop work keeps missing: an animation that starts after a
pause reallocates and refaults everything.  In the guest that measured
19.4 ms for a 16 MiB buffer allocated, mapped and written after 2.5 s of
idle, against 1.5 ms on the host.

So hold buffers for eight seconds when the device is virtio.  Bare metal
keeps the half second timeout.  pb_cache still enforces its own size
limit, so the longer timeout needs no further bound.

Only compile-tested: this was developed in a guest with an Intel host, and
measured there with the equivalent iris change.  The amdgpu numbers above
come from the same benchmark run on an RX 9070 XT host.
---
--- a/src/gallium/winsys/amdgpu/drm/amdgpu_winsys.c
+++ b/src/gallium/winsys/amdgpu/drm/amdgpu_winsys.c
@@ -35,6 +35,22 @@
 static struct hash_table *dev_tab = NULL;
 static simple_mtx_t dev_tab_mutex = SIMPLE_MTX_INITIALIZER;
 
+/**
+ * How long an unused buffer stays in the cache, on bare metal and behind a
+ * virtio-gpu native context.
+ *
+ * Dropping a cached buffer costs whatever the CPU mapping of its replacement
+ * costs, which on bare metal is about 0.2 us per 4 KiB page.  In a guest each
+ * page of a new mapping instead faults into a range the host GPU driver
+ * exposes, measured at about 4 us per page: first touching a new 16 MiB
+ * mapping takes 18 ms in the guest against 0.8 ms on the host.  A cache hit
+ * skips all of it.  So hold buffers long enough for work that resumes after a
+ * pause - an animation, a window being resized again - to reuse them rather
+ * than fault in a new mapping.  pb_cache still enforces its size limit.
+ */
+#define BO_CACHE_USECS 500000
+#define BO_CACHE_USECS_VIRTIO 8000000
+
 #if MESA_DEBUG
 DEBUG_GET_ONCE_BOOL_OPTION(all_bos, "RADEON_ALL_BOS", false)
 #endif
@@ -506,7 +522,8 @@
 
       /* Create managers. */
       pb_cache_init(&aws->bo_cache, RADEON_NUM_HEAPS,
-                    500000, aws->check_vm ? 1.0f : 1.5f, 0,
+                    is_virtio ? BO_CACHE_USECS_VIRTIO : BO_CACHE_USECS,
+                    aws->check_vm ? 1.0f : 1.5f, 0,
                     ((uint64_t)aws->info.vram_size_kb + aws->info.gart_size_kb) * 1024 / 8,
                     offsetof(struct amdgpu_bo_real_reusable, cache_entry), aws,
                     /* Cast to void* because one of the function parameters
